  {
    "title": "Nearly $1 Million in Funding Awarded to Local Communities, Reichard Says",
    "url": "https://repreichard.com/News/38696/Press-Releases/Nearly--1-Million-in-Funding-Awarded-to-Local-Communities--Reichard-Says",
    "date": null,
    "raw_text": "Nearly $1 Million in Funding Awarded to Local Communities, Reichard Says\nSeptember 15, 2026\nWAYNESBORO –\nRep. Chad Reichard (R-Franklin) announced that several communities in the 90th Legislative District received nearly $1 million in funding through Local Share Account (LSA) grants.\n“This funding will aid communities across the district with fulfilling vital, unique needs,” Reichard said. “I’m pleased to have supported projects that will have positive impacts on the lives of residents in the district.”\nThe following municipalities were awarded grants:\n•\tMercersburg Borough,\non behalf of the\nMercersburg Water Authority,\nreceived\n$500,000\nto install 30 valves and replace 493 outdated residential meters and a master meter to improve billing accuracy and deploy automated valve exercising equipment to ensure long-term system reliability.\n•\tAntrim Township,\non behalf of\nRescue Hose Company #1 Fire Engine,\nreceived\n$250,000\nfor the purchase of a new fire engine to replace its 25-year-old apparatus.\n•\tWaynesboro Borough,\non behalf of the\nWaynesboro Area YMCA,\nreceived\n$200,000\nto renovate its two gymnasiums, including upgrades to HVAC, electrical systems, roofing, flooring, doors, paint and other infrastructure.\n•\tWaynesboro Borough\nreceived\n$25,509\nto purchase 21 new TASER devices, replacing outdated models.\n“Mercersburg’s aging water system has reached a point where these upgrades are necessary to improve reliability and address deficiencies before they become larger and more costly problems,” Reichard said. “This investment will modernize critical infrastructure, allow the authority to better manage repairs and disruptions, and help ensure Mercersburg residents have a dependable water system.”\nThe grants were approved and awarded by the Commonwealth Financing Authority, which was established as an independent agency that holds fiduciary responsibility over the funding of programs and investments in Pennsylvania’s economic growth. LSA grants are derived from gaming revenues.\nRepresentative Chad Reichard\n90th Legislative District\nPennsylvania House of Representatives\nMedia Contact: Greg Gross\n717.260.6374\nggross@pahousegop.com\nRepReichard.com\n/\nFacebook.com/RepReichard\nShare\nSign Up to Receive Legislative Email Updates\nKeep up-to-date on the latest legislative and community news. Your email address will be used strictly for legislative purposes.\nEmail*\nZIP Code\nSubscribe",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "The Commonwealth Financing Authority approved Local Share Account grants totaling approximately $975,509 for four projects in Pennsylvania's 90th Legislative District. The grants will fund water system upgrades in Mercersburg, a new fire engine for Antrim Township, YMCA gymnasium renovations in Waynesboro, and new TASER devices for Waynesboro Borough. Rep. Reichard announced the grants but did not author the grant program; LSA funds are derived from gaming revenues and distributed by an independent state agency.",
      "action_type": "statement",
      "topics": [
        "infrastructure",
        "public_safety",
        "other"
      ],
      "spin_flag": true
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Reichard: $34,000 in Grants Awarded for Local History Preservation",
    "url": "https://repreichard.com/News/38665/Press-Releases/Reichard---34-000-in-Grants-Awarded-for-Local-History-Preservation",
    "date": null,
    "raw_text": "Reichard: $34,000 in Grants Awarded for Local History Preservation\nSeptember 14, 2026\nWAYNESBORO\n– Three Franklin County museums will receive $34,000 in state funding through the Pennsylvania Historical and Museum Commission Cultural and Historical Support Grant Program, Rep. Chad Reichard (R-Franklin) announced.\nThe Franklin County Historical Society and Renfrew Museum and Park each received $9,500 while the Conococheague Institute received $15,000.\n“Franklin County has a tremendous history, and we are fortunate to have organizations and volunteers who work every day to preserve and share it with the next generation. These grants will help our local museums continue that important work,” Reichard said.  “I’m pleased to see this funding come back to Franklin County and support the organizations that help keep our local history alive.”\nThe Conococheague Institute will use its grant for its paid student internship program. The program has provided experience and career growth for 18 students, many of whom have gone on to have full-time positions in their field since 2022. The Franklin County Historical Society will use its funding for general operating expenses. Renfrew Museum and Park will use its grant to improve its communications infrastructure and collection management capabilities by expanding its Wi-Fi footprint to cover additional park buildings and to buy equipment, including new laptops and tablets.\nThe purpose of the grant program is to provide general operating support to Pennsylvania\nmuseums and official county historical societies that are not directly supported by other state agency operating support programs.\nRepresentative Chad Reichard\n90th Legislative District\nPennsylvania House of Representatives\nMedia Contact: Greg Gross\n717.260.6374\nggross@pahousegop.com\nShare\nSign Up to Receive Legislative Email Updates\nKeep up-to-date on the latest legislative and community news. Your email address will be used strictly for legislative purposes.\nEmail*\nZIP Code\nSubscribe",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "Three Franklin County historical organizations received a combined $34,000 in state grants through the Pennsylvania Historical and Museum Commission's Cultural and Historical Support Grant Program. The funds will support a student internship program at the Conococheague Institute, general operations at the Franklin County Historical Society, and technology infrastructure upgrades at Renfrew Museum and Park. Rep. Reichard announced the grants but did not administer or allocate them himself.",
      "action_type": "award",
      "topics": [
        "education",
        "other"
      ],
      "spin_flag": true
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Reichard Bill to Protect Children, Strengthen Police Response to Indecent Exposure",
    "url": "https://repreichard.com/News/38616/Press-Releases/Reichard-Bill-to-Protect-Children--Strengthen-Police-Response-to-Indecent-Exposure",
    "date": null,
    "raw_text": "Reichard Bill to Protect Children, Strengthen Police Response to Indecent Exposure\nSeptember 1, 2026\nWAYNESBORO – Sparked by a reported indecent exposure incident in a Washington Township park, Rep. Chad Reichard (R-Franklin) will soon introduce legislation granting police the power to make an on-scene arrest during such cases.\nUnder current Pennsylvania law, police must witness indecent exposures firsthand to make an on-scene arrest without a warrant.\n“I was astonished to learn from police that an on-scene arrest couldn’t be made when a man was seen by others exposing himself near a park where children play,” Reichard said. “I quickly got to work to right this wrong and a bill to fix this issue will soon be introduced.”\nAccording to Washington Township Police, officers responded to Red Run Park for a reported indecent exposure incident. Though police gathered enough information indicating an alleged crime took place near the playground area, and officers deemed the offense met the level of a misdemeanor, officers could not make an on-scene arrest since they did not personally witness the act. Charges have since been filed.\nReichard’s forthcoming legislation would establish an exception for indecent exposure, allowing an officer to make an on-scene arrest when the officer has probable cause to believe the offense has been committed.\nState law already provides for warrantless arrests for certain misdemeanors, including offenses such as scattering rubbish. This legislation would similarly give police the authority to respond promptly and appropriately to allegations of indecent exposure.\n“Parents should be able to take their children to a local park without worrying that someone who exposes themselves can simply walk away because an officer didn’t happen to witness the act,” Reichard said. “Our police officers need the tools and authority to protect our communities. This legislation will help ensure they can take action when there is probable cause that an indecent exposure has occurred.”\nRepresentative Chad Reichard\n90th Legislative District\nPennsylvania House of Representatives\nMedia Contact: Greg Gross\n717.260.6374\nggross@pahousegop.com\nRepReichard.com / Facebook.com/RepReichard\nShare\nSign Up to Receive Legislative Email Updates\nKeep up-to-date on the latest legislative and community news. Your email address will be used strictly for legislative purposes.\nEmail*\nZIP Code\nSubscribe",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "Rep. Chad Reichard announced plans to introduce a bill that would allow police to make warrantless on-scene arrests for indecent exposure when they have probable cause, even if they did not personally witness the act. Current Pennsylvania law requires officers to witness the offense directly to arrest without a warrant. The bill has not yet been introduced.",
      "action_type": "bill_introduced",
      "topics": [
        "public_safety"
      ],
      "spin_flag": true
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Reichard to Host Workshop on Protecting Residents From Scams",
    "url": "https://repreichard.com/News/38546/Press-Releases/Reichard-to-Host-Workshop-on-Protecting-Residents-From-Scams",
    "date": null,
    "raw_text": "Reichard to Host Workshop on Protecting Residents From Scams\nAugust 19, 2026\nWAYNESBORO – With scammers constantly finding new ways to deceive and defraud victims, Rep. Chad Reichard (R-Franklin) is inviting area residents to a free workshop designed to help them recognize and avoid scams.\nReichard is partnering with Pennsylvania Attorney General Dave Sunday’s office to host the event Friday, Aug. 28, at 10 a.m. in the Hess Room at Quincy Village, 6596 Orphanage Road, Waynesboro.\n“Scammers are always looking for new ways to trick people, and advances in technology, including artificial intelligence, are giving them even more tools,” Reichard said. “This workshop will provide residents with information they can use to spot scams before they become victims. Knowing what to look for can make all the difference.”\nA representative from the Office of Attorney General will explain how scammers are using new technology and evolving their tactics to target victims. The presentation will cover common warning signs, including efforts to create a false sense of trust or urgency, as well as ways AI is being used to make scams more convincing.\nParticipants also will receive practical tips for protecting their personal information, financial accounts and other sensitive information.\n“Taking a few minutes to learn how these scams work could save someone from a major financial or personal loss,” Reichard said. “I hope residents will take advantage of this opportunity to become better informed and better prepared.”\nFor more information, contact Reichard’s office at 717-749-7384.\nRepresentative Chad Reichard\n90th Legislative District\nPennsylvania House of Representatives\nMedia Contact: Greg Gross\n717.260.6374\nggross@pahousegop.com\nRepReichard.com / Facebook.com/RepReichard\nShare\nSign Up to Receive Legislative Email Updates\nKeep up-to-date on the latest legislative and community news. Your email address will be used strictly for legislative purposes.\nEmail*\nZIP Code\nSubscribe",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "Rep. Chad Reichard is hosting a free public workshop on August 28, 2026 in Waynesboro, partnering with the Pennsylvania Attorney General's office to educate residents about scam recognition and prevention. The event will feature a presentation covering how scammers use technology, including AI, to deceive victims, along with practical tips for protecting personal and financial information.",
      "action_type": "event",
      "topics": [
        "public_safety",
        "other"
      ],
      "spin_flag": false
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Reichard Secures More Than $775,000 for Safer Schools",
    "url": "https://repreichard.com/News/38496/Press-Releases/Reichard-Secures-More-Than--775-000-for-Safer-Schools",
    "date": null,
    "raw_text": "Reichard Secures More Than $775,000 for Safer Schools\nAugust 14, 2026\nWAYNESBORO\n– Schools in the 90th Legislative District received $775,429 in funding from the Pennsylvania Commission on Crime and Delinquency’s (PCCD) School Safety and Security Committee (SSC) to improve safety for students, Rep. Chad Reichard (R-Franklin) announced.\n“I’m pleased to see our districts being proactive in promoting safety for children who attend our schools,” Reichard said. “This funding will assist districts with improving security for students so that they can learn in safe environments.”\nSchool districts in the 90th Legislative District to receive grants are:\n•\nFranklin County Career and Technology Center: $70,000.\n•\nGreencastle-Antrim School District: $224,239.\n•\nTuscarora School District: $196,110.\n•\nWaynesboro Area School District: $285,080.\nThe PCCD’s School Safety and Security Committee awards grants to schools and organizations across Pennsylvania to improve safety measures and provide essential mental health services for students and staff.\nRepresentative Chad Reichard\n90th Legislative District\nPennsylvania House of Representatives\nMedia Contact: Greg Gross\n717.260.6374\nggross@pahousegop.com\nShare\nSign Up to Receive Legislative Email Updates\nKeep up-to-date on the latest legislative and community news. Your email address will be used strictly for legislative purposes.\nEmail*\nZIP Code\nSubscribe",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "Four school districts in Pennsylvania's 90th Legislative District received a combined $775,429 in state grants through the Pennsylvania Commission on Crime and Delinquency's School Safety and Security Committee. The funds are designated for improving school security measures and mental health services. Rep. Reichard announced the grants but did not author the grant program.",
      "action_type": "statement",
      "topics": [
