  {
    "title": "Antrim Township Receives More Than $500,000 For Traffic Signal Improvements, Reichard Says",
    "url": "https://repreichard.com/News/38417/Press-Releases/Antrim-Township-Receives-More-Than--500-000-For-Traffic-Signal-Improvements--Reichard-Says",
    "date": null,
    "raw_text": "Antrim Township Receives More Than $500,000 For Traffic Signal Improvements, Reichard Says\nAugust 4, 2026\nHARRISBURG – Rep. Chad Reichard (R-Franklin) today announced that Antrim Township will receive more than $500,000 through Pennsylvania’s Green Light-Go Program, which aims to upgrade existing traffic safety infrastructure.\nThe Green Light-Go Program provides reimbursement grants to municipalities for projects that enhance safety, reduce congestion and keep traffic moving efficiently. This program is administered by the Pennsylvania Department of Transportation.\n“Keeping our roadways safe and efficient is essential to protecting our communities. These improvements will protect our drivers while positioning the township to meet future transportation needs. I’m grateful to see this investment being made in our region,” said Reichard.\nThe funding will support traffic signal modernization and safety improvements at the following intersections:\n•\tBuchanan Trail East (Pa. 16) and Grindstone Hill Road.\n•\tBuchanan Trail East (Pa. 16)/Antrim Church Road/John Wayne Drive.\n•\tMolly Pitcher Highway (U.S. 11)/Comfort Inn Driveway/I-81 northbound off ramp.\n•\tBuchanan Trail East (Pa. 16) and I-81 southbound off/on ramps.\n•\tBuchanan Trail East (Pa. 16) and I-81 northbound off/on ramps.\n•\tMolly Pitcher Highway (U.S. 11)/Antrim Commons Drive/I-81 southbound off/on ramps.\nRepresentative Chad Reichard\n90th Legislative District\nPennsylvania House of Representatives\nMedia Contact: Greg Gross\n717.260.6374\nggross@pahousegop.com\nShare\nSign Up to Receive Legislative Email Updates\nKeep up-to-date on the latest legislative and community news. Your email address will be used strictly for legislative purposes.\nEmail*\nZIP Code\nSubscribe",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "Antrim Township in Franklin County will receive over $500,000 from Pennsylvania's Green Light-Go Program to modernize traffic signals at six intersections, primarily along Route 16 and U.S. 11 near Interstate 81. The funding comes from a state reimbursement grant program administered by PennDOT. Rep. Reichard announced the grant but did not administer or vote on the funding.",
      "action_type": "statement",
      "topics": [
        "infrastructure",
        "public_safety"
      ],
      "spin_flag": true
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Reichard Working to Assist Those Affected by Recent Storm",
    "url": "https://repreichard.com/News/38399/Press-Releases/Reichard-Working-to-Assist-Those-Affected-by-Recent-Storm",
    "date": null,
    "raw_text": "Reichard Working to Assist Those Affected by Recent Storm\nJuly 30, 2026\nHARRISBURG\n- A storm on Tuesday wreaked havoc, dumping more than 4 inches of rain on the Waynesboro/Washington Township area, causing damage to roads and property. Since then, Rep. Chad Reichard (R-Franklin) has toured the area to see the damages and has been working with local and state officials to support residents to get their lives back on track.\nAs state representative, Reichard has been working to link local government officials with state agencies, such as Pennsylvania Emergency Management Agency (PEMA), to hasten assistance to affected residents.\n“My number one goal is to get assistance to those who need it quickly, efficiently and effectively,” Reichard said. “Local officials have been working non-stop to aid residents, and state officials were quick to respond in our time of need. I cannot thank them enough for all their hard work.”\nImpacted residents are encouraged to photograph and document all damage, and work with their insurance companies. Residents of Waynesboro can email\nfloodingreports@waynesboropa.gov\nto report damages so the borough can coordinate with PEMA and the Federal Emergency Management Agency. Damages can also be reported to the Franklin County Emergency Management coordinator at 717-264-2813.\nState and local officials are also out assessing damages. Residents can also call Reichard’s office at 717-749-7384 and he and his staff will help direct them to the appropriate entity for assistance.\nReichard discussed the damage and ways in which residents are being assisted on his recent podcast, which\ncan be found here\n. The audio can be downloaded and used for newscasts.\nRepresentative Chad Reichard\n90th Legislative District\nPennsylvania House of Representatives\nMedia Contact: Greg Gross\n717.260.6374\nggross@pahousegop.com\nShare\nSign Up to Receive Legislative Email Updates\nKeep up-to-date on the latest legislative and community news. Your email address will be used strictly for legislative purposes.\nEmail*\nZIP Code\nSubscribe",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "Following a storm that caused flooding and property damage in the Waynesboro and Washington Township area of Franklin County, Rep. Chad Reichard toured the affected area and is connecting local government officials with state agencies like PEMA to coordinate disaster assistance. He is directing residents to report damages through the borough email, county emergency management, or his own office, which will refer them to appropriate agencies.",
      "action_type": "statement",
      "topics": [
        "public_safety",
        "infrastructure",
        "other"
      ],
      "spin_flag": true
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Reichard Comments on Budget",
    "url": "https://repreichard.com/News/38249/Press-Releases/Reichard-Comments-on-Budget",
    "date": null,
    "raw_text": "Reichard Comments on Budget\nJuly 12, 2026\nHARRISBURG –\nRep. Chad Reichard (R-Franklin), member of the House Appropriations Committee, issued the following statement after voting against the 2026-27 state budget:\n“Although House and Senate Republicans succeeded in reducing Gov. Josh Shapiro’s original $53.3 billion spending proposal, the finalized $50.85 budget still spends more than taxpayers can afford. This budget defers payments of $1.2 billion until next year. Delaying payments doesn’t mean the debt goes away; it simply means we’ll have to come up with funding next year.\n“While this budget doesn’t tap the Rainy Day Fund or raise taxes, Pennsylvania cannot continue spending beyond its means without eventually asking taxpayers to make up the difference. I have long said the best path forward is to grow our economy and, in turn, grow our revenues without raising taxes.\n“We have seen some of the results of the positive aspects House and Senate Republicans fought to be included in last year’s budget. We saw to it that historic permitting reform, investments in career and technical training, leaving the Regional Greenhouse Gas Initiative and meaningful welfare reforms were included. The outcome was $1.14 billion more than the official revenue estimate and $2.52 billion more than the 2024-25 fiscal year.\n“Despite Republican initiatives leading to higher-than-expected revenue, that is not a reason for the new and increased spending in this budget. Lawmakers must realize a budget not just funds government operations for a year, it sets the course for years and decades to come. Pennsylvania must be on sound financial footing now, so we are on a prosperous path well into the future.”\nRepresentative Chad Reichard\n90th Legislative District\nPennsylvania House of Representatives\nMedia Contact: Greg Gross\n717.260.6374\nggross@pahousegop.com\nRepReichard.com\n/\nFacebook.com/RepReichard\nShare\nSign Up to Receive Legislative Email Updates\nKeep up-to-date on the latest legislative and community news. Your email address will be used strictly for legislative purposes.\nEmail*\nZIP Code\nSubscribe",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "Rep. Chad Reichard voted against Pennsylvania's 2026-27 state budget of $50.85 billion, citing concerns about overspending and a $1.2 billion deferred payment pushed to the following fiscal year. He issued a statement explaining his opposition, arguing that higher-than-expected revenues should not justify increased spending. He also credited Republican-backed provisions from the prior year's budget, such as permitting reform and welfare changes, for generating additional revenue.",
      "action_type": "statement",
      "topics": [
        "budget",
        "economy"
      ],
      "spin_flag": true
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Reichard Invites Residents to Anti-Scam Event",
    "url": "https://repreichard.com/News/38014/Press-Releases/Reichard-Invites-Residents-to-Anti-Scam-Event",
    "date": null,
    "raw_text": "Reichard Invites Residents to Anti-Scam Event\nMay 28, 2026\nHARRISBURG – To keep residents updated on the growing ways in which scammers target victims, Rep. Chad Reichard (R-Franklin) is teaming up with Pennsylvania Attorney General Dave Sunday’s office to host an anti-scam workshop.\nThe free, informative event will be held at 10 a.m. on Thursday, June 4, at the MMP&W Volunteer Fire Co. Activities Center, 20 Veterans Way, Mercersburg.\n“This presentation will hit on new scams and new technology being used by scammers,” Reichard said. “I encourage residents to attend so they can learn how not to become victims. The greatest tool to combat scammers is knowledge of their methods and being able to identify scams.”\nScammers are using new means and technology, such as AI, to target victims. A representative from the Pennsylvania Office of Attorney General will discuss new scams and methods scammers are using. This workshop will help attendees recognize scams and understand the tactics scammers use to build trust and urgency, and how they use AI to do so. Attendees will also learn practical ways to protect personal information and finances.\nFor more information about the event, call Reichard’s office at 717-749-7384.\nRepresentative Chad Reichard\n90th Legislative District\nPennsylvania House of Representatives\nMedia Contact: Greg Gross\n717.260.6374\nggross@pahousegop.com\nRepReichard.com / Facebook.com/RepReichard\nShare\nSign Up to Receive Legislative Email Updates\nKeep up-to-date on the latest legislative and community news. Your email address will be used strictly for legislative purposes.\nEmail*\nZIP Code\nSubscribe",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "Rep. Chad Reichard is co-hosting a free public workshop on scam awareness on June 4, 2026, in Mercersburg, PA, partnering with the Pennsylvania Attorney General's office. A representative from the AG's office will present information about current scam tactics, including AI-based methods, and how residents can protect themselves. The event is open to the public at no cost.",
      "action_type": "event",
      "topics": [
        "public_safety",
        "other"
      ],
      "spin_flag": false
    }
